Edition No. 37 — Saturday, 7 November 2026

10 stories: K284.8 billion and half a point on VAT: Musokotwane's 2027 budget taxes mining dividends at 15%, adds a 39.5% PAYE band and aims the deficit at 3% of GDP (lead); €60 million for Kapiri Mposhi–Ndola and the 1984 Railways Act to go: the budget opens the line to private trains, as Wonderful Group talks of US$1.2 billion for the southern line; Two contracts signed, 386 kilometres of line, power by 2029: Kapala tells Parliament the Tanzania interconnector is under way, and promises no load shedding, El Niño or not; A million hectares and more than US$100 million: the government asks the World Bank to hurry the Lobito Corridor forest project, with first activities pencilled in for January; 'You are wasting your time': Kalaba takes the EU observers' report and tells them to find a way to enforce it, as the church monitors say their own findings match the mission's; Three wards voted and the UPND took all three, eight contests never happened, and the President held his first rally since August: what Thursday's by-elections produced; Two more dates: Archbishop Banda's Hilux case goes to 25 November while the DPP answers a defence application, and the treason bail panel is reported to rule on 10 November; 'I have done nothing wrong apart from speaking for doctors': the RDAZ president is retired in the national interest, and two opposition leaders ask the Ministry of Health why; Blades back on top of Stream A a day after Nkana got there, Green Eagles two clear in Stream B with one goal conceded in six, and a goalless Lusaka derby at Sunset; A Nice midfielder, a Sheffield Wednesday forward and a point from two games: Burundi name 25 for the double-header against Zambia on 13 and 17 November.

